  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The bats went silent and never found them again. They scored one run on one hit and struck out eleven times, and by the time anyone in a Padres uniform had a chance to make it matter, Cleveland had already built a five-run lead in the seventh inning. This was not a game that slipped away. It was a game that was lost in the second inning and simply confirmed across the next six.",
    "what_this_means": "What this game revealed is uglier than the scoreline suggests. Wandy Peralta lasted one inning and left three runs on base for a bullpen that would spend the next seven frames cleaning up damage it never started. The offense did not just underperform; it vanished. Eleven strikeouts and one hit is not a slump. It is a locked door. The lineup found one runner in scoring position and converted it, but that single moment of execution could not survive in a game where the bats went cold after the first inning and never warmed again. One run on one hit across nine innings is not a strategy. It is surrender.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tanner Bibee arrives tonight with a 3.99 ERA facing a Padres lineup that struck out eleven times yesterday. Exactly the kind of matchup that rewards a starter who can paint and make hitters chase. If the bats show up empty again, tonight could look a lot like yesterday.",
